<ul>
    <li><strong>The existing way</strong> &mdash; subagents each work in an <strong>isolated environment</strong> and only return the result (in parallel). There was no way for them to talk to each other.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent team</strong> &mdash; the agents work <strong>while communicating</strong>, so they behave more smartly.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, suppose a research agent finished its investigation and terminated, then an analysis agent was spawned, and during the analysis it finds <strong>a part where the research is lacking</strong> &mdash; instead of doing the research itself, it <strong>wakes the research agent back up</strong> and asks for additional investigation.</p>

<h4>Enabling agent teams</h4>
<p>This feature is an <strong>experimental version</strong>, so it is disabled by default. Turn on the environment variable in <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> in your user folder.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># env in ~/.claude/settings.json</span>
"CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS": "1"</code></pre>
<ul>
    <li>Only when this value is on do the agents talk to each other and act as a team.</li>
    <li>When an agent <strong>succeeds at dynamic loading</strong>, team mode turns on properly; when it fails, it often comes up as an isolated ordinary subagent. (A bug of the experimental version.)</li>
    <li>For reference, agent-to-agent communication has also been added to Gemini's <strong>Antigravity CLI</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Context = the kitchen counter</h4>
<p>Context is like the <strong>counter</strong> on which you make a dish. It is tidy when only things related to that dish are on it, but once unrelated things get mixed in, there is room for mistakes (like sugar being in the salt shaker). In practice, when a conversation gets long the thinking before and after can end up <strong>contradictory</strong>, and if you give it work in that state the AI gets confused about "is A right or is B right". That is why <strong>context management</strong> matters.</p>

<h3>The Three Core Elements and How They Work</h3>
<p>There are many harness components, but the instructor takes the view that <strong>multi-agent collaboration happens well with just these three</strong> and focuses on them &mdash; <strong>agent · skill · orchestrator</strong>.</p>

<h4>1) Agent — a natural-language document</h4>
<p>An agent is not code but <strong>a document written in natural language</strong>. It holds the following.</p>
<ul>
    <li>The role and the <strong>principles</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Input protocol · output protocol</strong> (in what format to produce the result)</li>
    <li><strong>Collaboration</strong> &mdash; what to exchange with other agents</li>
    <li>Behavior on re-invocation and <strong>when an error occurs</strong></li>
</ul>

<h4>2) Skill — standardizing repeated work</h4>
<p>A skill is a <strong>name + description</strong> that freely holds a defined process, knowledge and checklist.</p>
<ul>
    <li>Because the process is defined, <strong>consistency</strong> is maintained.</li>
    <li>You can <strong>combine</strong> small skills to compose a bigger system.</li>
    <li>Thanks to the standardization, repeated work gets faster.</li>
</ul>
<p>A knack for designing skills: <strong>find the repeated work → separate it by single responsibility → define input/output → handle exceptions → connect it to an agent.</strong></p>

<h4>3) Orchestrator — a PM that does not micromanage</h4>
<p>It is the <strong>PM</strong> responsible for whether the whole workflow is carried out well. Unlike a failing PM it does not interfere in the details; it only checks something like "is the workflow being carried out well".</p>

